  "story": {
    "headline": "How to Watch the Total Solar Eclipse on a Live Stream, Witnessing the Rare View of the Sun's Corona From Your Own Home",
    "summary": "Wednesday’s eclipse will bring dark skies to parts of Russia, Greenland, Iceland, Portugal and Spain. But with these online broadcasts, you can view the spectacle from anywhere",
    "publisher": "Rally News",
    "url": "https://rally.news/categories/entertainment/?article=How%2Bto%2BWatch%2Bthe%2BTotal%2BSolar%2BEclipse%2Bon%2Ba%2BLive%2BStream%2C%2BWitnessing%2Bthe%2BRare%2BView%2Bof%2Bthe%2BSun%27s%2BCorona%2BFrom%2BYour%2BOwn%2BHome"
  },
  "researchBrief": "The research confirms that NASA and ESA are indeed providing live streams of the August 12th total solar eclipse. NASA's coverage begins at 1:15 p.m. EDT, with totality starting in Iceland at 1:45 p.m. and in Spain at 2:28 p.m. ESA's broadcast will be available on YouTube and ESA Web TV, starting at 19:30 CEST. Both organizations will feature expert commentary and high-quality imagery. The eclipse will be visible in parts of Greenland, Iceland, Spain, and a small corner of Portugal, with partial visibility in other parts of the Northern Hemisphere. The live streams ensure that people worldwide can experience this rare event safely and comfortably from home.",
